  The Social Sciences and Humanities Research Council of Canada
  (\acro{SSHRC}) requires applications to its Insight Grants program
  to be split into a multitude of plain-text and \acro{PDF} documents.
  This makes it challenging for teams of grant seekers to maintain
  consistency in formatting and cross-referencing, particularly when
  using word processors.  This article describes the development and
  use of a \LaTeX\ class for Insight Grants proposals,
  \tbcodebreak{sshrc-insight}.
  
  In addition to formatting the proposal according
  to \acro{SSHRC}'s specifications, the class allows authors to easily
  switch between producing a single, unified \acro{PDF} for the
  writing and internal review phase, and multiple \acro{PDF}s for
  final submission.  Additional features include stable citation
  numbering across compilation modes, automatic character counts for
  the plain-text parts, and French localization.
  
  Beyond providing an
  overview of the class and its intended workflow, this article
  describes the solutions to various technical challenges that were
  required to implement its features.  The aim is thus not only to
  introduce a practical tool for grant writers, but also to provide
  guidance to developers of classes for similar fragmented,
  portal-driven document workflows.
